question":"What is Paleophytology the study of?","difficulty":2,"explanation":"Paleophytology uses fossils to trace how vegetation changed through geological time.","alternatives":[{"id":"01a218f9-3720-75bc-b057-d5a7e7c75d15","description":"ancient multicelled plants","correct_answer":true},{"id":"01a218f9-3720-7d63-9e2d-3925bc37987a","description":"eggs","correct_answer":false},{"id":"01a218f9-3720-7c3b-9e16-053a8c10681a","description":"the study, especially statistical, of elections","correct_answer":false},{"id":"01a218f9-3720-7370-a019-743c1968ec5c","description":"viruses","correct_answer":false}]},{"id":"01a218f9-3720-7460-a5fe-f84bfbb8ca29","locale":"en-US","category":"01a21312-b489-7df1-b0c8-10e72868dcc8","question":"What is the other name for the Celsius temperature scale, meaning \"divided into one hundred parts\"?","difficulty":2,"explanation":"Centigrade refers to the 100-degree interval between water\u2019s freezing and boiling points at standard atmospheric pressure.","alternatives":[{"id":"01a218f9-3720-7191-9a08-c595a754da7c","description":"Centigrade","correct_answer":true},{"id":"01a218f9-3720-7278-a471-4fff6b416926","description":"Fahrenheit","correct_answer":false},{"id":"01a218f9-3720-769c-9a1c-a511eab30a84","description":"Kelvin","correct_answer":false},{"id":"01a218f9-3720-7b7b-a084-8d375dad6b70","description":"Rankine","correct_answer":false}]},{"id":"01a218f9-3720-7704-9587-30fc91eb34de","locale":"en-US","category":"01a21312-b489-7df1-b0c8-10e72868dcc8","question":"What adjective is attached to both Gustave Pasch\'s match and Walter Hunt\'s pin?","difficulty":2,"explanation":"Safety matches keep key chemicals apart until striking, while a safety pin\'s clasp covers its sharp point.","alternatives":[{"id":"01a218f9-3720-7126-9d28-d3cc80cdea8e","description":"Safety","correct_answer":true},{"id":"01a218f9-3720-7fe0-8ab6-12bf84fe08e2","description":"Sharp","correct_answer":false},{"id":"01a218f9-3720-7bea-bf6e-f35d82a100f7","description":"Smooth","correct_answer":false},{"id":"01a218f9-3720-73f5-b90f-161f0e50ad5b","description":"Bright","correct_answer":false}]},{"id":"01a218f9-3720-7920-a797-91eb09db3a21","locale":"en-US","category":"01a21312-b489-7df1-b0c8-10e72868dcc8","question":"What is the tallest tree species in the world?","difficulty":2,"explanation":"Coast redwoods can grow taller than 100 meters. Giant sequoias are generally shorter, though they are much bulkier.","alternatives":[{"id":"01a218f9-3720-74e6-947c-c66ab6dd38e6","description":"Coast Redwood","correct_answer":true},{"id":"01a218f9-3720-7ddd-bf16-5a00f125fd0c","description":"Mountain Ash","correct_answer":false},{"id":"01a218f9-3720-7a7c-90f4-53a567b2fe73","description":"Oak Tree","correct_answer":false},{"id":"01a218f9-3720-70d6-a5ef-46590061ed7a","description":"Douglas Fir","correct_answer":false}]},{"id":"01a218f9-3720-78aa-8277-2003d538b299","locale":"en-US","category":"01a21312-b489-7df1-b0c8-10e72868dcc8","question":"Which fish is able to breathe air?","difficulty":2,"explanation":"Lungfish have air-breathing organs, and some can survive drought buried in mud inside a mucus cocoon.","alternatives":[{"id":"01a218f9-3720-7e00-bce4-0fbf2d7d1aa7","description":"Lungfish","correct_answer":true},{"id":"01a218f9-3720-71ff-840c-c36d013c84cd","description":"Shark","correct_answer":false},{"id":"01a218f9-3720-706c-adba-759a2b2d359f","description":"Airfish","correct_answer":false},{"id":"01a218f9-3720-7b20-ab8b-7734969f9572","description":"Bubblefish","correct_answer":false}]},{"id":"01a218f9-3720-7179-acda-8ad510ea97f1","locale":"en-US","category":"01a21312-b489-7df1-b0c8-10e72868dcc8","question":"What are, on average, the heaviest primates?","difficulty":2,"explanation":"Gorillas are the largest living primates. The name silverback refers to the pale hair that develops across a mature male\u2019s back.","alternatives":[{"id":"01a218f9-3720-7281-b797-ca9440b2e9a3","description":"Gorillas","correct_answer":true},{"id":"01a218f9-3720-757a-a7b4-a8343c50739e","description":"Humans","correct_answer":false},{"id":"01a218f9-3720-73bf-a12b-035ebff319e6","description":"Orangutans","correct_answer":false},{"id":"01a218f9-3720-7b1a-a0a7-ba02670348c6","description":"Chimpanzees","correct_answer":false}]},{"id":"01a218f9-3720-7ae1-b804-e990d4bb7b80","locale":"en-US","category":"01a21312-b489-7df1-b0c8-10e72868dcc8","question":"Which is NOT a source of renewable energy?","difficulty":2,"explanation":"Petroleum forms over geological timescales from buried organic matter, so it is finite. Biomass, sunlight, and tidal motion are replenished naturally.","alternatives":[{"id":"01a218f9-3720-7b9b-bf80-7cb907ce51d5","description":"Petroleum","correct_answer":true},{"id":"01a218f9-3720-7367-8a5b-fbac7fa333b9","description":"Biomass","correct_answer":false},{"id":"01a218f9-3720-7eeb-9435-71fd26532f21","description":"Sun","correct_answer":false},{"id":"01a218f9-3720-788b-98ea-0ffa212b2218","description":"Tides","correct_answer":false}]},{"id":"01a218f9-3720-7b95-a075-e7b88fdb3c6c","locale":"en-US","category":"01a21312-b489-7df1-b0c8-10e72868dcc8","question":"If you were travelling at mach 2 how fast would you be travelling?","difficulty":2,"explanation":"Mach number compares an object\'s velocity with the local speed of sound, so Mach 2 is double that reference velocity.","alternatives":[{"id":"01a218f9-3720-773d-8a25-50b1946cfe3f","description":"Twice the speed of sound","correct_answer":true},{"id":"01a218f9-3720-723c-a2a2-8d3d823b2d06","description":"Twice the speed of light","correct_answer":false},{"id":"01a218f9-3720-76cc-a8ea-d7b034eac2bd","description":"200 miles per hour","correct_answer":false},{"id":"01a218f9-3720-7fa6-bc5c-b8582c6ba085","description":"2 miles per second","correct_answer":false}]},{"id":"01a218f9-3720-78fc-ad9b-45631e170d28","locale":"en-US","category":"01a21312-b489-7df1-b0c8-10e72868dcc8","question":"Which of these scientific theories was an improvement over the theory of mechanics by Isaac Newton?","difficulty":2,"explanation":"General relativity extends Newtonian gravity and predicts that massive objects bend light, an effect confirmed by observations near the Sun.","alternatives":[{"id":"01a218f9-3720-717a-b32c-25aa37e9fa29","description":"General relativity","correct_answer":true},{"id":"01a218f9-3720-7e8e-bd9e-df1ccc5910e3","description":"Lamarckism","correct_answer":false},{"id":"01a218f9-3720-71d6-b824-3e20f930eb76","description":"Maxwell\'s equations","correct_answer":false},{"id":"01a218f9-3720-7085-ba8e-0bd519b3c8ae","description":"Snell\'s law","correct_answer":false}]},{"id":"01a218f9-3720-7ef0-a6ea-50a653dbf733","locale":"en-US","category":"01a21312-b489-7df1-b0c8-10e72868dcc8","question":"How many neck vertebrae does a giraffe have?","difficulty":2,"explanation":"Seven is also the usual number of neck vertebrae in humans; giraffes\u2019 vertebrae are simply much longer.","alternatives":[{"id":"01a218f9-3720-7a2f-bb94-0a591f89a3a3","description":"Seven","correct_answer":true},{"id":"01a218f9-3720-7c8d-8305-aa855965fc2d","description":"Fourteen","correct_answer":false},{"id":"01a218f9-3720-7885-94cb-d4842e0e7087","description":"Nine","correct_answer":false},{"id":"01a218f9-3720-7a40-8334-3d6def33b109","description":"Twelve","correct_answer":false}]},{"id":"01a218f9-3720-796e-b032-8464caa87a8b","locale":"en-US","category":"01a21312-b489-7df1-b0c8-10e72868dcc8","question":"What is the word used when one tectonic plate moves under another?","difficulty":2,"explanation":"Subduction can create deep ocean trenches and volcanic arcs, and it is associated with powerful earthquakes.","alternatives":[{"id":"01a218f9-3720-7576-a2b3-40c4fb712960","description":"Subduction","correct_answer":true},{"id":"01a218f9-3720-78de-acef-237511e8b3b5","description":"Profession","correct_answer":false},{"id":"01a218f9-3720-7bb4-91d8-5ecfe27fb680","description":"Induction","correct_answer":false},{"id":"01a218f9-3720-7552-8bc0-b770a9cb29f4","description":"Transgression","correct_answer":false}]},{"id":"01a218f9-3720-76e0-9428-70e6d27d8d36","locale":"en-US","category":"01a21312-b489-7df1-b0c8-10e72868dcc8","question":"Where in the body is the smallest human muscle?","difficulty":2,"explanation":"The stapedius, the body\'s smallest skeletal muscle, is in the middle ear and helps limit the stapes\' movement in response to loud sounds.","alternatives":[{"id":"01a218f9-3720-70fa-91a6-4d84f45311cb","description":"Ear","correct_answer":true},{"id":"01a218f9-3720-71e3-9b46-87f5e53ae5b4","description":"Nose","correct_answer":false},{"id":"01a218f9-3720-76eb-adc5-a0e07a12a773","description":"Hand","correct_answer":false},{"id":"01a218f9-3720-7c39-87ce-75cc89aa4a95","description":"Foot","correct_answer":false}]},{"id":"01a218f9-3720-73b6-a7ef-7e7713afabec","locale":"en-US","category":"01a21312-b489-7df1-b0c8-10e72868dcc8","question":"What is the specialty of an ophthalmologist?","difficulty":2,"explanation":"Ophthalmologists are medical doctors who can perform surgery to treat conditions affecting the eyes.","alternatives":[{"id":"01a218f9-3720-716f-8139-6c26ba27aa72","description":"Eyes","correct_answer":true},{"id":"01a218f9-3720-73f0-bf54-a77d26b1b512","description":"Testes","correct_answer":false},{"id":"01a218f9-3720-791e-baf9-b97cf0aa0b40","description":"Kidneys","correct_answer":false},{"id":"01a218f9-3720-70e1-949d-8e2b9aff237f","description":"Feet","correct_answer":false}]},{"id":"01a218f9-3720-71a4-b2c4-d8762f941186","locale":"en-US","category":"01a21312-b489-7df1-b0c8-10e72868dcc8","question":"How many sides does a heptagon have?","difficulty":2,"explanation":"The prefix hepta- comes from Greek for seven and also appears in heptathlon, an event with seven disciplines.","alternatives":[{"id":"01a218f9-3720-736a-a834-ba7fe3ea5311","description":"7","correct_answer":true},{"id":"01a218f9-3720-7855-86c5-a8af0c017147","description":"8","correct_answer":false},{"id":"01a218f9-3720-7ced-aece-facc9df30e8c","description":"6","correct_answ